## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Turn the fleet landing page into an efficient cross-project navigation and review surface. Provide
global search over bounded reported identifiers and titles, useful type/status/freshness/version
filters, explainable sorting, stable filter URLs, compact and comfortable density options, and
optional maintainer-local pinned projects or saved views.

### Why It Matters

As more projects enroll, a flat list and four aggregate cards will force the operator to scan every
project manually. The dashboard should help answer "what changed, what needs me, and where should I
look next?" without requiring prior knowledge of a project's UUID or opening many tabs.

### Desired Outcome

The operator can search by project, stable artifact ID, bounded display title, type, lifecycle, or
version; filter to active, blocked, recently completed, unreconciled, stale, divergent, or
behind-version projects; and share or bookmark the exact resulting view. Fleet rows show the most
useful compact context: attention reason, current work, last meaningful change, last completion,
freshness, and version posture. Personal pins or saved views change presentation only and never
alter project truth or priority.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Artifact search depends on the privacy-bounded lifecycle inventory explored in `IDEA-0012`.
- Search only allowlisted bounded fields. Do not index document bodies, prompts, source, paths,
  commands, arbitrary diagnostics, or hidden local content.
- Preserve accessible keyboard navigation, focus state, narrow-screen behavior, and stable server
  routes. A full single-page application is not required.
- Saved views and pins are maintainer presentation preferences, not Tool Shed queue priority or
  ownership state.
- Do not add complex team sharing, role-based dashboards, or cross-account collaboration without
  an observed need.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| URL-backed server-side search and filters | Simple, bookmarkable, accessible, works without SPA state | Page reloads and query design | Preferred foundation |
| Client-only filtering of loaded rows | Fast interaction | Incomplete for pagination and large fleets | Useful only for small loaded sets |
| Per-maintainer saved views and pins | Faster repeated review | Adds preference storage and reset semantics | Add after core filters prove useful |
| Search arbitrary uploaded document text | Powerful | Violates minimum disclosure and expands indexing risk | Reject |

## Open Questions

- Which five filters provide the most value in the first useful release without creating a control
  panel full of rarely used options?
- Should bounded artifact titles participate in global search by default, or require an explicit
  project scope?
- Are pins sufficient, or do recurring operator workflows justify named saved views?
- What is the default explainable sort: attention severity, last meaningful change, or a combined
  operational-relevance key?
- How should pagination preserve project navigation and stable URLs?

## Decisions

- Build on `IDEA-0012` rather than creating a second artifact-history contract.
- Prefer server-rendered, URL-backed search and filters with accessible progressive enhancement.
- Keep pins and saved views presentation-only and defer them until the core fleet filters are
  proven useful.
- Reject arbitrary document-body indexing.

## Don't Forget

- Search should accept human stable IDs such as `IDEA-0012` and `PRM-0027`, not only UUIDs.
- "Operational relevance" must remain explainable; hidden scoring would make the dashboard harder
  to trust.
- Dense mode should increase information visibility without reducing keyboard or touch usability.
- Export belongs with bounded analytical/reporting views, not as a substitute for good navigation.

